\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 BBtools\

\fs24 \

\f1\b0\fs20 java-based tool set for filtering and transforming genomic data\
uses k-mers and other stuff\
\
\

\f0\b\fs24 Installation\
\

\f1\b0\fs20 create a conda environment with java >6 installed and activate it\
download from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sourceforge.net/projects/bbmap/"}}{\fldrslt https://sourceforge.net/projects/bbmap/}}
\f0\b \cf2 \
	
\f1\b0 \cf2 $ tar -xzf BBMAP*.tar.gz					extracts the files	\
	$ cd bbmap\
	$ .stats.sh in=resources/phix174_ill.ref.fa.gz		runs a test\
	$ ln -s $(pwd)/<tool>.sh ~/.local/bin/<tool>		create executable symlinks\
\
\
\

\f0\b\fs24 general usage\
\

\f1\b0\fs20 the scripts are wrappers around a java call \
all tools use general usage scheme:
\f0\b \cf2 \
	
\f1\b0 \cf2 $ <tool> in=<input> out=<output>				input and output files\
	$ <tool> in=<input> out=<output> ref=<genome.fasta>	some tools need reference genome\
if you have paired reads, use in1 and in2 to analyse paired fastq files together\
	$ <tool> in1=<fR1.fastq> in2=<R2.fastq> out1=<output1> out2=<output2\
the tools autodetect file extensions (also for compression) for input and output files\
\
for piping, the stdin and stdout have to be set with the proper extension (autodetect does not work in piping):\
	$ cat <R1.fastq.gz> | <tool> in=stdin.fastq.gz out=stdout.fa int=f		\
		int=f/t tells the program whether files are interleaved paired end files\
\
